<div class="collapsible-block">
<div class="collapsible-block-folded"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">[+]&nbsp;Open&nbsp;for&nbsp;crit</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded" style="display:none">
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded-link"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">–&nbsp;hide&nbsp;block</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-content">
<blockquote>
<ul>
<li>The child unknowingly manifests horrors that protect her in her sleep, but only in the event she has a nightmare.</li>
<li>The manifestation of these monsters will attack any other lifeform that comes within 3 meters of her as she slumbers.</li>
</ul>
</blockquote>
<p>I get like, the child manifesting horrors to protect herself from nightmares but my concern here is it feels weird to mix like&#8230;dream terrors with real protection? Like, why doesn't she create dream monsters that exist solely in her dreams to protect her? Obviously that wouldn't really be a SCP then but it feels&#8230;weird</p>
<p>Also if the monsters are protecting her, why are they hostile to all lifeforms (which might be a bit broad btw, like <a href="/scp-3749">SCP-3749</a> shows, 'all lifeforms' includes 'bacteria' of which there are thousands in the air) and not just like, hostile lifeforms? Or even unknown lifeforms: it feels like if e.g. her mother approached, they wouldn't attack?</p>
<blockquote>
<p>Through trial and error the researchers assigned to her case begin to understand her condition, learn how to avoid potential manifestations of her monstrous protectors, and attempt to rehabilitate her in the process.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>So like, narratively what does this actually look like? My concern is that it feels like it could be very passive, but also like it might not be that interesting to read? It really depends on how this conflict plays out and stuff.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>I want to make sure it's air tight before I even consider sending in a draft for serious critique.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Just noting here that you'll need two greenlights on the concept before you can use the Drafts Critique forum to get crit.</p>
</div>
