				<div class="info">
					<span class="printuser avatarhover"><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/daliesque" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(7043295); return false;"  ><img class="small" src="http://www.wikidot.com/avatar.php?userid=7043295&amp;amp;size=small&amp;amp;timestamp=1606078127" alt="Daliesque" style="background-image:url(http://www.wikidot.com/userkarma.php?u=7043295)"/></a><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/daliesque" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(7043295); return false;" >Daliesque</a></span> <span class="odate time_1606058782 format_%25e%20%25b%20%25Y%2C%20%25H%3A%25M%7Cagohover">22 Nov 2020 15:26</span>
														</div>
			</div>
			<div class="content" id="post-content-4825554">
									

<blockquote>
<p>I get like, the child manifesting horrors to protect herself from nightmares but my concern here is it feels weird to mix like…dream terrors with real protection? Like, why doesn't she create dream monsters that exist solely in her dreams to protect her? Obviously that wouldn't really be a SCP then but it feels…weird</p>
</blockquote>
<p>I can see how that would feel weird! I should have been more specific but I was trying not to ramble on. I established that the child has C-PTSD, but I should have been more clear as to why.</p>
<p>I've loosely based the manifestation of her anomaly on &quot;An American Haunting,&quot; if you've ever seen it. The narratives of the two children are quite different, but their situations are very similar. It's an incredibly heavy topic, and I want to make sure I write it respectfully.</p>
<p>The neglect by her mother, and unspeakable abuse by her father has caused the child to live her formative years in a near constant state of fear, and panic. Repeated physical, emotional, and sexual abuse she is unable to comprehend led to these frequent nightmares. After some time, her suffering was so intense that she began to lose herself in the nightmares, because they were less terrifying than her real life. Those nightmares then became a shield for her, and eventually a literal weapon.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>Also if the monsters are protecting her, why are they hostile to all lifeforms (which might be a bit broad btw, like SCP-3749 shows, 'all lifeforms' includes 'bacteria' of which there are thousands in the air) and not just like, hostile lifeforms? Or even unknown lifeforms: it feels like if e.g. her mother approached, they wouldn't attack?</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Oh wow, I hadn't even considered just how broad that could be. I suppose to be more specific I should condense it down to humanoid lifeforms and entities. Thank you for pointing that out!</p>
<p>As far as why it attacks all (decidedly humanoid) lifeforms, in my head she's been through so much struggle and pain at such a young age, especially by those who were meant to protect her, her brain perceives <em>all</em> beings other than herself a potential threat. While the child usually chooses flight mode, her monsters always choose fight.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>So like, narratively what does this actually look like? My concern is that it feels like it could be very passive, but also like it might not be that interesting to read? It really depends on how this conflict plays out and stuff.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>That is my biggest fear - that it wont be interesting to read. My idea was to narrate the process and discovery of her origin through incident reports, interview logs, addendums and such, while trying to make it concise enough to be reasonably palatable. My goal is make the readers feel sympathy for the child, while reasonably portraying The Foundation and the interactions between them.</p>
<p>Thank you so much for responding by the way! I appreciate it greatly.</p>

							</div>

								
					
